    <section class="takeaways" aria-labelledby="takeaways-title">
      <h2 id="takeaways-title">Key takeaways</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Rome grew at a crossing of the Tiber, on a frontier between Latins, Etruscans and Greeks, and borrowed from all of them.</li>
        <li>Romans told their origins through Aeneas and through Romulus and Remus. These stories were written down centuries later and taught duty more than they recorded fact.</li>
        <li>Archaeology shows slow growth: hut villages on the hills, then a drained valley that became a shared public space.</li>
        <li>Tradition counts seven kings; the list is part memory, part invention, and reads like a set of examples.</li>
        <li>By tradition, in 509 BC Rome expelled Tarquin the Proud after the rape of Lucretia, and replaced the king with two consuls elected for one year.</li>
        <li>The Republic’s founding idea was power divided, limited in time, and accountable: the beginning of the <em>res publica</em>.</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="summary">This is the opening of the fourth ring, the country. The next lesson gives that ring its virtue: <em>pietas</em>, in Virgil’s Aeneas.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="takeaways" aria-labelledby="failed-title">
      <h2 id="failed-title">What failed, and what the next age learned</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Kingship without restraint.</strong> The tradition remembers the last king as a man who ruled without the Senate and treated citizens’ households as his own. Carried forward: power must be divided, limited in time and answerable. Rome’s two annual consuls were its first answer, and America’s Founders studied it.</li>
        <li><strong>A state for the few.</strong> The early Republic replaced one king with a narrow circle of patrician families. Carried forward: a republic must widen its circle or face conflict from within. The plebeians’ long struggle for a share, the subject of the next chapter, is that lesson in action.</li>
        <li><strong>A city that could be sacked.</strong> The Gallic sack showed that liberty without strength is not safe. Carried forward: Rome built walls, reorganized its army and bound its neighbors to it in alliances, the start of the road from city to country.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="word-day" aria-labelledby="glossary-title">
      <h2 id="glossary-title">Glossary</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Patria:</strong> fatherland; the country to which a Roman owed duty.</li>
        <li><strong>Pietas:</strong> duty to gods, family and country; the virtue of this chapter.</li>
        <li><strong>Res publica:</strong> “the public thing”; the Roman state, and the root of <em>republic</em>.</li>
        <li><strong>Latium:</strong> the region of central Italy where the Latins lived, and where Rome stands.</li>
        <li><strong>Etruscans:</strong> a people of central Italy north of Rome, whose cities, art and religion strongly influenced early Rome.</li>
        <li><strong>Palatine:</strong> the hill above the Tiber where tradition placed Romulus’ city and archaeology finds Iron Age huts.</li>
        <li><strong>Forum:</strong> the valley between Rome’s hills, drained and paved to become the center of public life.</li>
        <li><strong>Cloaca Maxima:</strong> “the greatest drain,” which carried water from the Forum to the Tiber.</li>
        <li><strong>Rex:</strong> king; after 509 BC a hated title in Rome.</li>
        <li><strong>Consul:</strong> one of two chief magistrates elected each year in the Republic.</li>
        <li><strong>Senate:</strong> the council of elders, made up of leading men and former magistrates.</li>
        <li><strong>Patricians and plebeians:</strong> the old aristocratic families, and the commoners.</li>
        <li><strong>Fasces:</strong> a bundle of rods and an axe, carried before magistrates as a symbol of their power.</li>
        <li><strong>Lapis Niger:</strong> “black stone”; a black-paved shrine in the Forum, beneath which lies one of the oldest Latin inscriptions.</li>
      </ul>
